"Emotion : a very short introduction / Dylan Evans. | Wellcome Collection (https://wellcomecollection.org/works/nu56zdw4)\nciteturn584search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; \n# Emotion : a very short introduction / Dylan Evans.\n\n  * Dylan Evans\n\nDate:\n    2003\n\n  * Books\n\n## About this work\n\n### Publication/Creation\n\nOxford : Oxford University Press, 2003.\n\n### Physical description\n\n140 pages : illustrations ; 18 cm.\n\n### Series\n\n  * Very short introductions\n\n### Contributors\n\n  * Dylan Evans\n\n### Notes\n\nOriginally published as: Emotion - the science of sentiment : Oxford : Oxford University Press, 2001.\n\n### Languages\n\n  * English\n\n## Subjects\n\n  * Emotions\n  * Emotions (Philosophy)\n\n## Where to find it\n\n  * Location  | Status  |  | \n--- | --- | --- | ---\nMedical Collection BF531 2003E78e  | Open shelves  |  | \n\n## Identifiers\n\n### ISBN\n\n  * 0192804618--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmotion: A Very Short Introduction - Dylan Evans - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books?id=OZOwDwAAQBAJ)\nciteturn584search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; \n\n# Emotion: A Very Short Introduction\n\nDylan EvansOxford University Press, Sep 26, 2019 - Psychology - 152 pages Was love invented by European poets in the Middle Ages or is it part of human nature? Will winning the lottery really make you happy? Is it possible to build robots that have feelings? In this Very Sort Introduction Dylan Evans explores these and many other intriguing questions in this guide to the latest thinking about the emotions. Drawing on a wide range of scientific research, from anthropology and psychology to neuroscience and artificial intelligence, Evans takes the reader on a fascinating journey into the human heart, discussing the evolution of emotions and their biological basis, the science of happiness, and the role that emotions play in memory and decision making. Greeted by critics as a pop science classic when it was first published in 2001, the book has now been thoroughly revised and updated to incorporate new developments in our understanding of emotions, including new sections addressing the neural basis of empathy and the emotional impact of films. ABOUT THE SERIES: The Very Short Introductions series from Oxford University Press contains hundreds of titles in almost every subject area. These pocket-sized books are the perfect way to get ahead in a new subject quickly. Our expert authors combine facts, analysis, perspective, new ideas, and enthusiasm to make interesting and challenging topics highly readable. [Input]\n\n### Selected pages\n\nPage 67\n\nPage 28\n\nTitle Page\n\nTable of Contents\n\n### Contents\n\nWhat is an emotion?  | 1\n | \nThe evolution of emotion  | 20\n | \nHow to be happy  | 42\n | \nThe head and the heart  | 62\n | \nThe computer that cried  | 79\n | \nThe heart has its reasons  | 96\n | \nReferences  | 101\n | \nFurther reading  | 107\n | \n\nIndex  | 111\n | \nA Very Short Introduction  | 117\n | \nA Very Short Introduction  | 118\n | \nA Very Short Introduction  | 119\n | \nA Very Short Introduction  | 120\n | \nA Very Short Introduction  | 121\n | \nA Very Short Introduction  | 122\n | \nCopyright  | \n\n### About the author (2019)\n\nDylan Evans received his PhD in Philosophy from the London School of Economics in 2000. He has taught at various universities around the world and written nine books, including The Utopia Experiment (Picador, 2015) and Atheism: All That Matters (Hodder 2014). In 2006-7 he ran the Utopia Experiment, an extended simulation of what life might be like if civilisation collapsed. He has also spent extended periods of time training horses in Guatemala, living with the Bedouins in the Sahara, and learning to play dead-goat polo in Kazakhstan.\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | Emotion: A Very Short Introduction\nVery Short Introductions\nAuthor  | Dylan Evans\nEdition  | 2\nPublisher  | Oxford University Press, 2019\nISBN  | 0192571230, 9780192571236\nLength  | 152 pages\nSubjects  | Psychology › Emotions\nPhilosophy / General\nPsychology / Emotions\nPsychology / Social Psychology\nScience / General\n   |  \nExport Citation  | BiBTeX EndNote RefMan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCiNii Books - Emotion : a very short introduction (https://ci.nii.ac.jp/ncid/BB2884079X)\nciteturn584search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\n# Emotion : a very short introduction\n\n  * Related Books: 1\n\n## Author(s)\n\n  *     * Evans, Dylan\n\n## Bibliographic Information\n\nEmotion : a very short introduction\n\nDylan Evans\n\n（Very short introductions, 81）\n\nOxford University Press, 2019\n\n2nd ed\n\n## Available at 44 / 44 libraries\n\n## Note\n\nIncludes bibliographical references and index\n\nPrevious ed.: 2003\n\n## Related Books： 1-1 of 1\n\n  *   *   *   *   *   *     * [Button: 1]\n    * [Button: 2]\n\n## Details\n\n  * NCID\n    BB2884079X\n  * ISBN\n    \n    * 9780198834403\n  * LCCN\n    2019941402\n  * Country Code\n    uk\n  * Title Language Code\n    eng\n  * Text Language Code\n    eng\n  * Place of Publication\n    Oxford\n  * Pages/Volumes\n    xxii, 116 p.\n  * Size\n    18 cm\n  * Classification\n    \n    * LCC : BF531\n    * DC23 : 152.4\n  * Subject Headings\n    \n    * LCSH : Emotions\n    * LCSH : Emotions (Philosophy)\n  * Parent Bibliography ID\n    \n    * BA34547828--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCreate your own economy : (https://lawcat.berkeley.edu/record/79253)\nciteturn584search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; \n\n# Create your own economy : the path to prosperity in a disordered world / Tyler Cowen.\n\nCowen, Tyler.\n\n2009\n\nAuthor\n\nCowen, Tyler.\n\nTitle\n\nCreate your own economy : the path to prosperity in a disordered world / Tyler Cowen.\n\nImprint\n\nNew York : Dutton, [2009]\n\nCopyright\n\n©2009\n\nDescription\n\nviii, 259 pages ; 24 cm\n\nFormatted Contents Note\n\nThe future of thinking differently\nHidden creativity\nWhy modern culture is like marriage, in all its glory\nIM, cell phones, and Facebook\nThe Buddha as savior and the professor as shaman\nThe new economy of stories\nHeroes\nBeauty isn't what you think\nAutistic politics\nThe future of the universe.\n\nSummary\n\nThe coauthor of the \"best economics blog in the universe\" offers a guide to success in a radically new hyper-networked age. The way we think is changing more rapidly than it has in a very long time. Not since the Industrial Revolution has a man-made creation--in this case, the World Wide Web--so greatly influenced the way our minds work and our human potential. Cowen argues that we are breaking down cultural information into ever-smaller tidbits, ordering and reordering them in our minds (and our computers) to meet our own specific needs. He explains why the coming world of Web 3.0 is good for us; why social networking sites such as Facebook are so necessary; what's so great about \"tweeting\" and texting; how education will get better; and why politics, literature, and philosophy will become richer.--From publisher description.\n\nBibliography, etc. Note\n\nIncludes bibliographical references and index.\n\n\nSTA\n\nCall Number\n\nK487.E3 C69 2009\n\nLanguage\n\nEnglish\n\nISBN\n\n9780525951230 hardback\n0525951237 hardback\n\nRecord Appears in\n\nMonographs & Serials--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEditions of Emotion: A Very Short Introduction by Dylan Evans (https://www.goodreads.com/work/editions/19341175-emotion-the-science-of-sentiment?expanded=false)\nciteturn584search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 25.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\n# Emotion > Editions\n\nexpand details\n\n## by Dylan Evans First published June 28th 2001\n\nEditions\n\nS