shed: 17.4 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; \n\nImage: cover image Create Your Own Economy: The Path to Prosperity in a Disordered World\n\nBUY THIS BOOK\n\n# Create Your Own Economy: The Path to Prosperity in a Disordered World\n\n### Tyler Cowen, . . Dutton, $25.95 (259pp) ISBN 978-0-525-95123-0\n\nImage\n\nIn this provocative study of behavioral economics, Cowen (Discover Your Inner Economist ) reveals that autistic tendencies toward classification, categorization and specialization can be used as a vehicle for understanding how people use information. Cowen spends a great deal of time dispelling autism’s societal stigma, arguing that “mainstream society is reaping benefits from mimicking autistic cognitive strengths.” As stimulating as is the premise, the book often feels like its own long exercise in categorization, with each chapter an analysis of the human mania for classification (e.g., the obsession with ranking achievements and endeavors). According to Cowen, human brains are constantly absorbing bits of information that get smaller and are delivered faster as technology advances. The more information people receive, the more they crave—this shorter attention span is far from a flaw to the author, but a liberating mechanism that allows humans time to contemplate more ambitious, long-range pursuits. The relentless analysis is occasionally overwhelming, but Cowen’s illustration of our neurological filing system may help readers understand the mass consumption of information and just about everything else. (July)\n\nDETAILS\n\nBUY THIS BOOK close\n\nDetails\n\nReviewed on: 05/18/2009\n\nGenre: Nonfiction\n\nCompact Disc - 979-8-200-12112-0\n\nMP3 CD - 979-8-200-12113-7\n\nOpen Ebook - 1 pages - 978-1-4001-9219-9\n\nOpen Ebook - 978-1-101-10858-1\n\nPre-Recorded Audio Player - 978-1-61574-863-1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJunk to Gold: From Salvage to the World’s Largest Online Auto Auction - Willis Johnson - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books/about/Junk_to_Gold.html?id=czKeAgAAQBAJ)\nciteturn584search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \n\n# Junk to Gold: From Salvage to the World’s Largest Online Auto Auction\n\nWillis JohnsonWestBow Press, Jan 14, 2014 - Business & Economics - 188 pages JUNK TO GOLD: How I Turned One Scrapyard into a Multi-Billion Dollar Global Business is the remarkable true story of Willis Johnson, founder of Copart [CPRT] and a self-made American entrepreneur. From humble beginnings on a family farm to building one of the largest online vehicle auction companies in the world, Willis shares an inspiring journey shaped by grit, faith, and hard work. Told with honesty and humility, JUNK TO GOLD blends personal stories of love, war, and family with hard-won lessons in business leadership. Willis reveals how his experiences—from serving in Vietnam to working alongside his father—laid the foundation for a multi-billion-dollar company. Through determination and resilience, he grew a single scrapyard into a global enterprise, never losing sight of his family-first values. At its core, JUNK TO GOLD is more than a business memoir. It’s a blueprint for anyone who dreams of building something bigger than themselves. Whether you're an aspiring entrepreneur, a business leader, or someone seeking inspiration, Willis’s story proves that with hard work, unwavering principles, and a clear vision, success is within reach. His journey will inspire you to believe in your own potential—and to start building your dream. [Input]\n\n### Selected pages\n\nPage 61\n\nPage 46\n\nPage 47\n\nPage 68\n\nPage 64\n\n### Contents\n\nLessons My Dad Taught Me  | 1\n | \nLessons the War Taught Me  | 17\n | \nLessons I Learned While Starting a Family  | 27\n | \nLessons I Learned Building a Dream  | 45\n | \nLessons I Learned as a Teacher  | 81\n | \nLessons I Learned Going to Wall Street  | 92\n | \n\nLessons I Learned from the Competition  | 113\n | \nLessons I Learned from the Next Generation  | 127\n | \nLessons I Learned from Our Employees  | 144\n\n### About the author (2014)\n\nBorn in Clinton, OK and raised on a farm in Arkansas, Willis Johnson grew up learning about business from his father, an entrepreneur who dabbled in everything from dairy farming to running restaurants to building houses. Willis would go on to breathe the same air of determination and perseverance. Willis’ first company Mather changed the way the auto parts industry operated and he would go on to do the same with Copart which started as one auto auction in Vallejo, California. More than 30 years later, Willis is Chairman of the Board of a multi-billion dollar business and Copart is the global leader in online auto auctions with over 160 locations worldwide.\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | Junk to Gold: From Salvage to the World’s Largest Online Auto Auction\nAuthor  | Willis Johnson\nPublisher  | WestBow Press, 2014\nISBN  | 1490816585, 9781490816586\nLength  | 188 pages\nSubjects  | Business & Economics › Industries › Automobile Industry\nBiography & Autobiography / Business\nBusiness & Economics / Entrepreneurship\nBusiness & Economics / Industries / Automobile Industry--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAUTHOR WISE CATALOGUE 110 (https://ghardalibrarybandra.org/authorwise.pdf)\nciteturn584search12 [wordlim: 200] \n<tr><td>164</td><td>Evan, Douglas</td><td>Freedom and stability in the world economy</td><td>330</td><td>EVA</td><td>B07310</td><td>5A</td><td>230</td><td>1976</td></tr>\n<tr><td>165</td><td>Evan, William M.</td><td>Organization theory</td><td>301.1</td><td>EVA</td><td>B06985</td><td>6A</td><td>312 p</td><td>1976</td></tr>\n<tr><td>166</td><td>Evanovich, Janet</td><td>To the Nines</td><td>823</td><td>EVA</td><td>B03227</td><td>S-3</td><td>341p</td><td>2003</td></tr>\n<tr><td>167</td><td>Evans, Dylan</td><td>Emotion : A Very Short Introduction</t